[Date "2022.08.23"]
[Round "1"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Slav / Schlechter"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Schlechter Variation"]
[SourceTitle "Tearing Down The Slavic Wall | Slav Defense | The Sensei Speedrun | GM Naroditsky"]
[SourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WmCImz1fv5s"]

1. Nf3 c6
2. d4 d5
3. c4 Nf6
4. Nc3 g6
5. cxd5 cxd5
6. Bg5
{Weak-deck reference. Against the fianchetto Slav, use `Bg5` to make development awkward and prepare e3/Bd3 or Qb3 pressure.}
6... Bg7
7. e3 O-O
8. Bd3 Nc6
9. O-O
{Naroditsky's broad Slav lesson: develop actively first, then choose the central break. Do not rush pawn grabs if Black's development is lagging.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Quiet Slav Bf5"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WmCImz1fv5s"]
[Date "2022.08.23"]
[Round "2"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Quiet Slav"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Quiet Variation, Schallopp Defense"]

1. Nf3 d5
2. d4 Nf6
3. c4 c6
4. e3 Bf5
5. Nc3
{Weak-deck reference. Develop the knight before choosing whether to chase the bishop or build slowly.}
5... e6
(5... Bg4
{Naroditsky points out that premature bishop activity in Slav structures often walks into Ne5 ideas.}
6. Qb3 Qb6
7. Ne5
{Centralize the knight and hit c6/b7 before Black consolidates.})
6. Nh4
{The reference correction: ask the f5-bishop where it is going.}
6... Bg6
7. Nxg6 hxg6
8. Bd3 Nbd7
9. O-O Bd6
10. h3
{Plan: trade or chase the active bishop, castle, and only then open the center with e4 or cxd5 when your pieces are ready.}
*

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Classical Slav dxc4"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WmCImz1fv5s"]
[Date "2022.08.23"]
[Round "3"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Classical Slav"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Czech / Classical"]

1. Nf3 d5
2. d4 Nf6
3. c4 c6
4. Nc3 dxc4
5. a4
{Stop `...b5` and prepare to recover c4.}
5... Bf5
6. e3
(6. Ne5
{Wiesbaden-style weak-deck branch.}
6... e6
7. Nxc4
{After recovering the pawn with the knight, look for e3, Be2/Bd3, and central development rather than pawn hunting.})
6... e6
7. Bxc4
{Weak-deck reference. Get the pawn back, then develop.}
7... Bb4
8. O-O O-O
9. Qe2
{Classical Slav summary: `a4` is not decorative; it stops easy `...b5`. After Bxc4, play for development and central breaks.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Geller Gambit"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WmCImz1fv5s"]
[Date "2022.08.23"]
[Round "4"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Slav Geller Gambit"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Geller Gambit"]

1. Nf3 d5
2. d4 Nf6
3. c4 c6
4. Nc3 dxc4
5. e4
{The energetic option. Naroditsky's Slav lesson repeatedly returns to the same idea: if Black grabs c4 and delays development, White can hit the center.}
5... b5
6. a4
{Weak-deck reference. Undermine the pawn chain before Black fully protects c4.}
6... a6
7. axb5 cxb5
8. Nxb5
{Open the queenside and use development. If Black overextends with `...b5`, the c5 and b5 squares become targets.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Chebanenko and Advance"]
